# Copy and locale

User-facing strings live in `COPY` in `lib/i18n.ts`. The `Copy` type is the contract. Add a key to the type and to both `en` and `es` in the same change. Components read `useLocale().copy`. Do not hardcode a sentence in a component.

Locale is `"en"` or `"es"`. `resolveLocale()` prefers the `usbordertime.lang` cookie, then the first `Accept-Language` tag. A tag starting with `es` selects Spanish. `localeStore` stays `null` until the user picks a language, so the server choice remains. After a pick, the cookie and `usbordertime.lang.v1` in localStorage both update. A signed-in account also saves that choice on `preference`.

Appearance is `"light"` or `"dark"`. Until the user picks one, the page follows `prefers-color-scheme` and nothing is stored. `resolveTheme()` reads the `usbordertime.theme` cookie. `themeStore` stays null until a pick, then that cookie and `usbordertime.theme.v1` both update. A signed-in account also saves that choice on `preference`. A blocking script in the root layout sets `data-theme` from the cookie before paint. Do not rename either key without a migration.

CBP text stays as published. Construction notices, hours, and port status are not translated. `waitLabel`, `presentHours`, and `presentPortStatus` only wrap those values.

A wait stays in minutes until it reaches an hour, then it is hours and minutes: `45 min`, `1 hr`, `1 hr 30 min` (Spanish `45 min`, `1 h`, `1 h 30 min`). `formatDuration` builds those words. `WaitDuration` draws the number on a card.

A label change needs a look at the screen in both languages, including a crossing that is open, one that is closed, and one that does not offer the lane on screen.
